#
# Assets upstream of this one that currently have an unresolved fault.
#
# PARAMETERS: ?asset -- bound by the caller to the equipment showing a symptom.
#
# This is the query cross-asset diagnosis is built on, and it is the whole reason
# the chilled water loop edge in building_extensions.ttl had to exist. The air
# handler's coil failing to hit its supply air temperature is a symptom. If a
# chiller upstream of that coil is already known to be faulted, the coil is
# probably a consequence rather than a fault of its own, and writing it up as an
# air handler problem sends someone to the wrong machine.
#
# HOW THE FAULT STATE GETS HERE. The graph is loaded from files and holds no
# fault state of its own. The caller in model/graph.py asserts mvn:hasOpenFault
# on the affected assets into a throwaway copy of the graph, runs this, and
# discards the copy -- so no fault ever becomes part of the persisted model and
# there is no stale state to invalidate.
#
# Where the caller gets its list is deliberately not this file's business, and
# right now the answer is that nothing produces one yet: the rule engine that
# writes detected faults is Task 3. Note what it must NOT be. The labelled fault
# events in the groundtruth schema are unreadable from here by design -- the role
# this code connects as has every grant on that schema revoked, so a SELECT
# against it fails outright. Faults reaching this query have to have been detected,
# not looked up.
#

PREFIX brick: <https://brickschema.org/schema/Brick#>
PREFIX mvn:   <https://aqueduct-pdm.local/mvn#>

SELECT DISTINCT ?upstream ?upstream_class ?fault
WHERE {
    ?asset (^brick:feeds)+ ?upstream .
    ?upstream mvn:hasOpenFault ?fault .
    OPTIONAL { ?upstream a ?upstream_class }
}
